# Runtime dependencies — loose bounds, hand-edited, documents INTENT.
# The lock is a SINGLE requirements.txt pinning the whole environment (runtime
# and dev together): pip freeze reports one environment and cannot attribute a
# transitive dependency back to the .in file that pulled it in.
# The reproducible install is requirements.txt, fully ==-pinned, regenerated by
# `make lock`. Never hand-edit requirements.txt; never pin here.

# --- Dataframes (D5: polars) -------------------------------------------------
# EB-NeRD is native Parquet with list columns (article_ids_inview,
# article_id_fixed). Polars treats nested lists as first-class where pandas
# degrades them to object dtype; the official ebnerd-benchmark starter code is
# polars; lazy scan keeps ebnerd_small comfortably inside 30 GB RAM.
# The feature store is written as plain Parquet, so any engine can read it.
polars>=1.0,<2.0
pyarrow>=16.0

# --- Numerics ----------------------------------------------------------------
numpy>=1.26,<3.0
scipy>=1.11
scikit-learn>=1.4          # oracle for AUC/nDCG in tests; slice utilities

# --- Semantic retrieval (Q3) -------------------------------------------------
# D2: local-first, GPU optional. Encoders are inference-only, base size, fp16,
# seq<=128.
#
# The extra index is REQUIRED, not a preference. On Linux the default PyPI
# `torch` wheel is the CUDA build: it pulls ~2-3 GB of torch plus separate
# nvidia-* CUDA runtime packages, for ~5 GB installed. That is a heavy and
# slow dependency for a grader who will run this on CPU anyway, and `make setup`
# has to finish in a reasonable time on an ordinary connection.
#
# (An earlier version of this comment claimed torch resolves to the CPU wheel by
# default. That is true on macOS and false on Linux, which is what this repo
# targets. Verified by watching pip fetch torch-2.13.0-manylinux_2_28_x86_64.)
#
# To use the GPU locally instead, install the CUDA build over the top:
#   .venv/bin/pip install torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu121
# Nothing in the code changes: `--device auto` detects CUDA and falls back to CPU.
--extra-index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
torch>=2.2
sentence-transformers>=3.0
transformers>=4.40
faiss-cpu>=1.8             # ANN index; CPU build is sufficient at this scale
# Declared DIRECTLY, not left transitive: scripts/download_models.py imports it
# to pin the encoder to an immutable commit sha (Q3 D1, OQ-9). A direct import
# resting on someone else's transitive pin breaks silently when they drop it.
huggingface_hub>=0.24

# --- Re-ranking (A2 Q2) -------------------------------------------------------
# GBDT option named explicitly in the assignment ("LightGBM/XGBoost"). LightGBM
# chosen over XGBoost: pure pip install (no separate native toolchain step on a
# grader's machine), and its `lambdarank` objective takes a per-impression
# group directly, matching the "one impression = one ranking group" shape the
# feature table is already built in.
lightgbm>=4.0

# --- Lexical retrieval (Q2) ---------------------------------------------------
# Snowball stemmers for the Q2.9 tokenisation ablation: `porter` for English,
# `danish` for Danish. Chosen over NLTK because it is pure Python, ~100 KB, and
# ships the algorithms IN the wheel -- no `nltk.download()` at install time, so
# `make setup` stays offline-reproducible.
#
# Needed only by the `en_stem` / `da_stem` variants. The headline baselines do
# not stem, and `tokenise.py` imports this lazily and raises rather than
# degrading to no-stemming if it is absent -- a silent fallback would report the
# baseline's numbers as the ablation's.
#
# The matching Danish STOPWORD list is not in this package (it ships stemmers
# only); it is vendored at src/newsrec/retrieval/stopwords/da.txt from the same
# Snowball project, so the ablation is one coherent change (OQ-8).
snowballstemmer>=3.0

